$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/sidecar
$type: service
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-26"
summary: >
  Sidecar / Resolver — the only component permitted to resolve a capability token. It validates
  tokens, requests data-key unwrap from the key store, injects plaintext into a non-LLM consumer,
  and zeroises. Written in Go, it runs as a separate user from agents with seccomp/AppArmor
  confinement and no shared memory.
$system: smith-gray/system
name: Sidecar / Resolver
status: built
type: worker
runtime: go
port: 0
paths:
  - apps/sidecar
depends_on:
  - control-plane
  - key-store
tech_stack:
  language: Go
  crypto: AES-256-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 for object encryption; signed capability tokens
  transport: Unix domain socket for local agent/CLI requests
  isolation: separate OS user, seccomp/AppArmor profile, no shared memory with agents
  observability: Prometheus metrics (resolution latency, deny rate)
dev:
  command: go run ./apps/sidecar/cmd/sidecar
  url: ""
  env:
    SAFEKEYS_SOCKET: /run/safekeys/sidecar.sock
    CONTROL_PLANE_URL: http://localhost:8080
    CONTROL_PLANE_API_KEY: dev-only-api-key
prod:
  url: ""
  region: ""
  deployment_ref: ""
testing:
  framework: Go testing
  command: go test ./apps/sidecar/...
  coverage_target: 80%
security:
  auth_method: Local authenticated channel; control-plane API key; device identity via mTLS later
  secrets_ref: docs/help/security-model.md
modules:
  - name: token-verify
    purpose: Verify token signature, expiry, audience, scope, and jti denylist before any unwrap
    paths:
      - apps/sidecar/internal/verify
  - name: resolve
    purpose: Request DEK unwrap from the key store and decrypt the target object in memory
    paths:
      - apps/sidecar/internal/resolve
  - name: inject
    purpose: Injection adapters — env var, 0600 temp file, Unix socket/pipe, exec wrapper
    paths:
      - apps/sidecar/internal/inject
  - name: zeroise
    purpose: Best-effort zeroisation of DEK and plaintext buffers after use
    paths:
      - apps/sidecar/internal/zeroise
  - name: socket
    purpose: Local request/response protocol over the Unix domain socket
    paths:
      - apps/sidecar/internal/socket
risks:
  - >-
    A rooted host can observe the sidecar's process memory — the MVP relies on process isolation
    only; Phase 1 adds secure element / TEE / TPM binding.
  - >-
    Long-running sidecar process holds transient DEKs in memory; minimise lifetime and zeroise
    deterministically.
future:
  - Secure element / TPM / cloud enclave backend for the long-term key share.
  - Threshold reconstruction of key material inside the element.
decisions:
  - id: go-sidecar
    decision: Implement the sidecar in Go rather than Rust.
    rationale: >-
      Shares one toolchain with the control plane and CLI, offers strong process-isolation
      primitives (seccomp, cgroups, distinct UID), and lowers the contribution barrier for an
      open-source reference implementation. The blueprint listed "Rust or Go"; Go keeps the MVP path
      shorter.
    alternatives:
      - option: Rust
        rejected_because: >-
          Strongest memory-safety story for the most privileged component, but introduces a second
          toolchain alongside Go with no MVP-blocking security gain once process isolation and
          zeroisation are implemented.
    status: accepted
  - id: separate-os-user
    decision: The sidecar runs as a different OS user from any agent process, with no shared memory.
    rationale: >-
      The architectural promise is that compromise of the agent sandbox does not yield the key; user
      separation plus memory isolation is the mechanism in the software-only phase.
    status: accepted
  - id: deny-by-default
    decision: If the key store or control plane is unreachable, resolution fails closed.
    rationale: A fallback to cached or plaintext values would violate the core security property.
    status: accepted
